WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
- Companies in the S&amp;P 500
are on pace to buy back

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:06.330
more than $1 trillion worth
of their own stock this year,

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:07.320
a record high.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.690
Warren Buffett recently
defended stock buybacks,

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:12.870
or at least insisted that
they're not always bad,

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:15.420
but these companies are buying
record amounts of their stock

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:18.570
at a time when investors are
largely selling their shares.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.730
I'm going to explain why
companies are buying back

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:21.870
their stock right now,

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:25.230
what stock buybacks are and
how they benefit corporations,

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.270
and why this could be a risky gamble

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.490
for some of the market's big names.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:30.540
Let's get into it.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.360
Goldman Sachs recently
authorized $30 billion

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.740
of planned stock buybacks,

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.870
and Facebook parent Meta announced plans

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.150
to buy $40 billion of their own stock.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.460
This might sound like
an odd use of capital

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.770
given that these companies'
stock prices have sunk

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:46.590
and they've recently announced
thousands of layoffs.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:49.200
Data show that both
institutional investors

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.240
and retail investors
have been selling stock

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:52.260
in recent weeks.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.710
But companies like to buy back
stock during market selloffs

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:57.180
for a number of reasons.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:00.240
First, seeing the company
buy signals to investors

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.000
that management still sees
the stock as attractive.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.920
It shows they're putting
some skin in the game.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.650
Second, with growing
uncertainty in the market,

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.750
investors are saying
buybacks are what they want.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:12.210
Bank of America's latest
Global Fund Manager Survey

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:13.650
shows the percentage of investors

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.140
who want companies to
return cash to shareholders

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.090
is at the highest since 2017.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:21.420
Third, the latest earnings
results show S&amp;P 500 companies

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.490
are generating lower net profits

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:25.260
and seeing their pricing power decline.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.820
By purchasing their own stock,

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.160
companies can improve their
earnings per share numbers,

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.620
making the stock more
attractive to investors.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:33.420
When you calculate earnings per share,

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.810
lower earnings don't look so
bad if there are fewer shares.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.400
It's also important to note

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.340
that top executives' pay
packages are often tied

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:42.900
to the company's stock price.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:47.130
So a higher stock price often
means more money for CEOs

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:50.220
and others who decide how to
deploy a company's capital.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.050
But what are stock buybacks,

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:54.540
and why do companies do
them in the first place?

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:55.890
When companies go public,

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:58.080
they sell shares to anyone who wants them.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.000
When companies buy back their stock,

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.160
they literally buy available shares

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.430
from market makers or
individual shareholders.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:08.070
Buybacks have historically
increased a stock's price

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:11.100
because, as our economics
textbook teaches us,

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:14.310
when supply decreases and
demand stays the same,

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:16.230
prices typically rise.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:19.140
But buybacks are not without their risks.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.990
Big companies like Meta and
Goldman have lots of cash

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:23.310
on their balance sheets,

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:26.160
but others could run into
some serious trouble.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.620
With interest rates rising,
it will be much more expensive

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:30.180
for companies to borrow money.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.490
Some firms could find
themselves running outta cash,

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.230
while still needing to pay employees,

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.390
vendors, and suppliers.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:37.890
That could create a cash crunch

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:40.020
where companies have to sell off assets

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.520
or declare bankruptcy.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.730
So when companies announce
buybacks, some investors buy

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.400
because it usually means
the price will be rising,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.980
but some sell because it can also mean

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:51.780
that the company is getting desperate

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.150
and has more concern with its stock price

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:56.649
than with investing in the company itself.

00:02:56.649 --> 00:02:59.232
(upbeat music)

